This group-relative signal is common in reinforcement learning and preference-based post-training pipelines. Rather than treating every sample independently, the trainer compares them against one another and updates the model so that higher-scoring behaviors become more likely and lower-scoring behaviors become less likely. In practice, this helps the model learn to prefer responses that are more useful, accurate, or aligned with the desired task.
